// (ratified 2026-08-17, card #2018): the standard library declares
// the conversion for its own error family. A program can write `run: fn()` and
// pass a Core failure up automatically without declaring anything, because
// these declarations already sit on the existing rail. One source;
// every tier lowers the same `impl <CoreError> -> Err` block, the same way
// `Prelude/Units.jet` ships one dimension catalog.
//
// Sema injects only the conversions a module actually exercises — fallible
// calls whose error type is a family member converting into the default `Err`.
// Unused members are not copied into the module, so a defect in one conversion
// cannot break a program that never produces that error.
//
// An author who wants the failure named still writes the named form
// (`run: fn() !EncodingError`), and that stays correct: this file only makes the
// unnamed form work.
//
// This rule keeps the rest of the rule intact. A user error type still
// needs its own `impl MyError -> Err { … }`; nothing here weakens that. A module
// that declares its own conversion for one of these pairs wins over the shipped
// one, so a package can still say how its program reports a Core failure.
//
// Family membership is derived, not chosen by taste. A Core error type belongs
// here when all three hold:
//   1. the Prelude registers it printable — `auto_printable` in
//      `TraitRegistry::register_synthetic_display_debug`, so the Prelude already
//      owns its failure text;
//   2. a Core signature returns it in the error position of a `T !E` result, so
//      a program can actually receive one; and
//   3. showing it is not prohibited.
//
// `CryptoError` and `FileCryptoError` are members. Both
// are payload-free enums that name only which check failed, so reporting the
// case carries no secret. The secret-bearing types (`Secret`, `SigningKey`,
// `X25519SecretKey`, `SharedSecret`) keep their E0915 print ban.
//
// `[FieldError]`, the list every Core typed decode fails with, is a member
// with no declaration here: a written conversion source is one named type, so
// sema injects its routine (`return Err("{self}")`) under the element's name
// and binds the list as `self` (FailureLaw.jet sema_core_error_family_source).
//
// Deliberately outside the family:
// * `TaskFailure` — gives cancellation and deadline their own
//     carrier and report; it is not a library error family member.
//   * component types (`DataErrorKind`, `EncodingCause`, `IOContext`,
//     `NetErrorDetail`, `NetDNSError`, …) — no public Core call fails with one.

// Original compiler-owned declarations. These private records are ordinary
// checked nominal types; only Err carries the existing default-error role.
// Fresh construction evaluates the declared metadata default in this owner.
// Copy, context and conversion retain the existing stored metadata instead.
__jet_ErrorContextFrame: struct {
    text: String
    file: String
    line: Int
}
__jet_ErrorConversion: struct {
    source: String
    target: String
}
__jet_ErrorField: struct {
    name: String
    value: String
}
__jet_ErrorSpan: struct {
    start: Int
    end: Int
}
__jet_ErrorDetails: struct {
    variant: String
    fields: [__jet_ErrorField]
    source_span: __jet_ErrorSpan?
}
__jet_ErrorMetadata: struct {
    typed_identity: String?
    context_frames: [__jet_ErrorContextFrame]
    conversion_history: [__jet_ErrorConversion]
    details: __jet_ErrorDetails?
}
Err: struct {
    message: String
    code: String?
    cause: Err?
    __jet___memo_error_metadata: __jet_ErrorMetadata{__jet_ErrorMetadata{
        typed_identity: None,
        context_frames: [__jet_ErrorContextFrame]{},
        conversion_history: [__jet_ErrorConversion]{},
        details: None,
    }}
}

// A refused allocation reports without allocating: the message is static
// text, and the requested bytes stay on the AllocError a handler sees.
impl AllocError -> Err {
    return Err("memory allocation refused")
}

impl AppError -> Err {
    return Err("{self}")
}

